          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"ibsen-et-dukkehjem-003dfd3f3df5ffc6\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Rank’s rhetorical question reiterates and explicitly avows that he has loved Nora as deeply as anyone else has. Its core force is strongly truth-conditional: the interrogative framing challenges Nora’s judgment rather than casting doubt on his love. It does not itself undertake a commitment, and nothing indicates that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The perfect form can sound retrospective, but the surrounding confession and Rank’s continuing offer of devotion present the love as enduring and currently relevant.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Rank directly addresses Nora in a rhetorical question that restates and clarifies his immediately preceding confession of love.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rank has just indirectly disclosed his love by saying he would gladly give his life for Nora and had sworn that she should know before he died. After Nora calls his conduct horrid, he reformulates the disclosure explicitly: the objection, he asks, cannot be that he has loved her deeply. Nora confirms that the problem is not his feeling but his telling her, because the disclosure destroys the uncomplicated friendship on which she had intended to rely. His declaration therefore functions principally as a sincere avowal, despite its relational consequences and his accompanying offers of total assistance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“At jeg har elsket Dem fullt så inderlig som noen annen? Var det stygt?” (“That I have loved you just as deeply as anyone else has? Was that horrid?”)\",\"supports\":\"The first-person perfect construction explicitly presents Rank’s deep love for Nora as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Rank says that Nora is not loved only by Torvald, that he would gladly give his life for her, and that he had sworn she should know before he went away.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes the highlighted words as the explicit restatement of a deliberate confession rather than an involuntary expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Nora replies: “Nei, men at De går hen og sier meg det” (“No, but that you should go and tell me so”).\",\"supports\":\"Nora distinguishes Rank’s loving state from his act of revealing it, confirming that the highlighted core is understood as an avowal of love; the disruptive disclosure context does not create a separate core category.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is annotated as affirmative perfect, with the exact match “jeg har elsket Dem fullt så inderlig.”\",\"supports\":\"The grammatical metadata reinforces the reading of the clause as an affirmative presentation of a loving state.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. The rhetorical-question form, deliberate confession, and awkward relational consequences are adequately represented as contextual features and create no residual pressure for P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
